Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool used to create, manage, and update infrastructure automatically using code. Instead of manually creating resources like servers, networks, or storage from the cloud console, Terraform allows us to define everything in a configuration file. When we run Terraform commands, it reads the code and creates the infrastructure automatically. Terraform is developed by HashiCorp and it supports many cloud platforms like Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform.

Terraform helps DevOps engineers manage infrastructure in a simple and automated way. It reduces manual work, keeps infrastructure consistent, and allows teams to track infrastructure changes through version control systems like Git. Another benefit is that infrastructure can be recreated anytime using the same code, which makes deployments faster and more reliable.

Terraform is needed because managing cloud resources manually becomes difficult when there are many servers, databases, networks, and storage services. With Terraform, all infrastructure is written as code, which means it can be reused, shared with teams, and easily modified. It also provides automation, which is very important in DevOps workflows.

Terraform uses a language called HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language). HCL is a simple and human-readable language used to describe infrastructure. It looks similar to JSON but is easier to read and write. Terraform configuration files usually have the extension .tf.

Example of a simple Terraform configuration file:

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-123456"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

In Terraform configuration files, everything is written inside blocks. A block is a container that defines a specific infrastructure component or configuration. Blocks organize the Terraform code and tell Terraform what needs to be created.

Example of a block:

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
}

Here resource is the block type, "aws_instance" is the resource type, and "example" is the name of that resource.

Inside blocks we define parameters and arguments. These are used to configure how the resource should behave.

A parameter is the name of a setting or configuration option that Terraform supports.
An argument is the value given to that parameter.

Example:

instance_type = "t2.micro"

Here:

parameter → instance_type
argument → "t2.micro"

This means we are telling Terraform that the EC2 instance type should be t2.micro.

Before using Terraform, we must install it on our system. Terraform can be installed on Linux servers like Ubuntu.

Steps to install Terraform on Ubuntu:

wget -O - https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list
sudo apt update
sudo apt install terraform

After installation we can verify Terraform by running:

terraform -version

If Terraform is installed correctly, it will show the installed version.

To use Terraform with AWS, we must configure the AWS provider. The provider allows Terraform to communicate with AWS services and create resources.

Example provider configuration:

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

This tells Terraform to create resources in the AWS region us-east-1.

Terraform can also create storage services like an S3 bucket in AWS. An S3 bucket is used to store files, backups, logs, and application data in the cloud.

Example Terraform code to create an S3 bucket:

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "mybucket" {
  bucket = "my-terraform-demo-bucket"
}

Explanation:

resource defines the infrastructure component.
aws_s3_bucket is the resource type used to create an S3 bucket.
mybucket is the name of the resource inside Terraform.
bucket is the parameter, and "my-terraform-demo-bucket" is the argument.

To run Terraform and create resources we use a few important commands.

First command:

terraform init

This command initializes the Terraform project and downloads required providers.

Second command:

terraform plan

This command shows what Terraform will create or change before actually applying it.

Third command:

terraform apply

This command creates the infrastructure defined in the Terraform configuration file.

If we want to delete the infrastructure created by Terraform, we use:

terraform destroy

This command removes all resources that were created using Terraform.

Terraform is widely used in DevOps because it allows teams to manage infrastructure using code. It improves automation, reduces manual errors, and makes cloud infrastructure easier to manage. With Terraform, the same configuration can be used to create identical environments for development, testing, and production. This makes deployments consistent and reliable.